HB.add({
  id: "01",
  intro: `Start here. Every RAG system ends with an LLM writing the answer, and every limit you will meet later — context windows, latency, cost, hallucination, why prompts work — comes from how these models are built. This section explains the Transformer, tokens and positions, attention and its fast variants, the kinds of models, how text is generated token by token, and how models are served efficiently. You do not need to train a model to build RAG, but you need this to make good design decisions and to answer interview questions with confidence.`,
  groups: [
    { id: "1.1", title: "Transformer Architecture", items: [
      { t: `Transformer (Encoder-Decoder)`,
        s: `The 2017 neural-network design ("Attention Is All You Need") behind every modern LLM, built from stacked attention and feed-forward layers.`,
        h: `The original Transformer has two stacks. The <b>encoder</b> reads the whole input at once and turns each token into a context-aware vector. The <b>decoder</b> writes the output one token at a time; each step looks at the tokens it already wrote (self-attention) and at the encoder's vectors (cross-attention). Every layer is attention followed by a small feed-forward network, wrapped with residual connections and normalisation. Because all input tokens are processed in parallel, it trains much faster than older recurrent networks.`,
        u: `Encoder-decoder Transformers are still used for translation, summarisation and text-to-text tasks (T5, BART, many machine-translation systems). More importantly, the same building blocks — attention, feed-forward layers, residuals — are reused by decoder-only chat models and encoder-only embedding models. Knowing the original design helps you understand every model family you will meet in a RAG stack: the embedder, the reranker and the answer-writing LLM.`,
        p: `Before Transformers, language models were recurrent networks (RNNs, LSTMs) that read text one word at a time. They were slow to train because they could not be parallelised, and they forgot information from far back in the text, so long documents and long-range references ("it" referring to something 200 words earlier) were handled badly. Attention lets every token connect directly to every other token, and parallel training made it possible to train on huge amounts of data — the step that led to today's LLMs.`,
        e: `A translation service sends the English sentence "The leave policy changed in April" to a T5-style model. The encoder reads all six words together and builds vectors that already know "policy" belongs to "leave". The decoder then writes the French sentence word by word, at each step attending to the encoder's vectors to decide what comes next ("La", "politique", "de", "congés"...). The same pattern — read with attention, write with attention — is what your RAG answer model does internally.` },
      { t: `Decoder-only Models (GPT-style)`,
        s: `Models that use only the decoder stack and are trained to predict the next token — the design of all chat LLMs.`,
        h: `The prompt and the answer are one continuous sequence of tokens. Causal attention lets each token see only the tokens before it. Training simply asks the model to predict the next token at every position of huge amounts of text. At generation time the model predicts one token, appends it, and repeats until it produces an end token or hits the length limit. Every task — answering, summarising, coding — is phrased as "continue this text", which is why one model can do so many things.`,
        u: `Every answer-writing LLM in RAG is decoder-only: GPT models, Llama, Qwen, Mistral, Gemma, Claude, Gemini. You also use them for query rewriting, routing decisions, LLM-as-judge evaluation, and agents that call tools. When you choose a model for your RAG answers, you are choosing among decoder-only models of different sizes, context lengths and licences. They are also the models you fine-tune with LoRA when you need a specific answer style, and the ones you size against your hardware.`,
        p: `Older systems needed a separate trained model for each task (one for classification, one for summarisation, one for Q&amp;A). A single decoder-only model trained on next-token prediction and then instruction-tuned can do all of them from a prompt, which made building assistants practical. It also explains a key weakness: the model produces likely-sounding continuations, not verified facts, so without grounding it can hallucinate confidently.`,
        e: `In this platform the prompt "Sources: [1] Every employee receives 24 days of paid annual leave… Question: How many leave days do I get? Answer:" goes to qwen2.5:3b. The model predicts "Every" → "full-time" → "employee" → … one token at a time, each prediction conditioned on the sources and everything it already wrote, until it emits the end token after "[1]." The streaming you see in the chat window is literally this token-by-token loop.` },
      { t: `Encoder-only Models (BERT-style)`,
        s: `Models that use only the encoder stack: they read the whole text at once in both directions and output vectors, not new text.`,
        h: `Every token attends to every other token, left and right (bidirectional attention). Training usually hides some words and asks the model to fill them in (masked-language modelling), so it learns rich context. The output is one vector per token; pooling them (mean or the [CLS] token) gives one vector for the whole text, or a small head on top gives a score or label. They cannot generate long text, but they understand text very efficiently.`,
        u: `In RAG, encoder-only models do the "understanding" jobs: embedding models for dense retrieval (e5, bge, gte), cross-encoder rerankers (ms-marco MiniLM, bge-reranker), and classifiers for intent, PII or toxicity. They are small (tens to hundreds of millions of parameters), fast on CPU, and cheap to fine-tune, which makes them ideal for steps that run on every chunk or every candidate. They also power language detection and NER in ingestion pipelines.`,
        p: `Using a large generative LLM for every embedding or relevance score would be slow and expensive, and often not better. Encoders read text in both directions at once, so they produce strong representations for search and classification at a fraction of the cost. Without them, scoring 30 rerank candidates or embedding a million chunks would need GPUs and much more time. They also fine-tune well on a few thousand labelled examples, which makes domain adaptation of retrieval affordable.`,
        e: `This platform uses two encoder-only models: <code>multilingual-e5-small</code> turns each chunk and each question into a 384-number vector for Qdrant, and <code>ms-marco-MiniLM-L6-v2</code> reads "question + chunk" pairs and outputs a relevance score for reranking. Both run on the CPU. Only the final answer is written by a decoder model (qwen2.5:3b). A typical interview question is exactly this: "which models in your RAG stack are encoders and which are decoders, and why?"` },
      { t: `Self-Attention`,
        s: `Each token looks at all the other tokens and rebuilds its own meaning as a weighted mix of them.`,
        h: `For every token the model computes three vectors: a query, a key and a value. It compares the token's query with every token's key (dot product, scaled by √d), turns the scores into weights with softmax, and outputs the weighted sum of the value vectors. Tokens that are relevant to each other get high weights. This happens in every layer, so meaning is refined many times: early layers capture grammar, later layers capture facts and reasoning patterns.`,
        u: `Self-attention is the core of every Transformer you use in RAG — embedders, rerankers and LLMs. Understanding it explains practical behaviour: why a cross-encoder reranker is more precise than a bi-encoder (question and chunk tokens attend to each other), why long prompts are slow (every token attends to every other), and why a fact placed far away in the context can still be used (attention connects any two positions directly).`,
        p: `Language is full of long-range links: pronouns, references to earlier sections, conditions stated far from the rule they modify. Recurrent models passed information step by step and lost it over distance. Self-attention connects every pair of tokens in one step, so "it" can directly pick up meaning from "the trophy" 30 words earlier, and the model can relate a question to the right sentence in a long source passage.`,
        e: `In "The trophy didn't fit in the suitcase because it was too big", the token "it" gives a high attention weight to "trophy" and a low one to "suitcase", so its new vector carries the meaning "the trophy". In a RAG prompt, when the model writes "carry over", attention lets it look back at the source sentence "Unused days carry over up to 5 days" and copy the number 5 correctly instead of guessing. This is also why a cross-encoder reranker beats a bi-encoder: its question tokens attend directly to the chunk tokens.`,
        c: `
import numpy as np
def attention(Q, K, V):                         # (n, d) each
    scores = Q @ K.T / np.sqrt(Q.shape[-1])     # similarity of every pair of tokens
    w = np.exp(scores - scores.max(-1, keepdims=True))
    w /= w.sum(-1, keepdims=True)               # softmax: weights per row sum to 1
    return w @ V                                # each token = weighted mix of values` },
      { t: `Multi-Head Attention`,
        s: `Several attention operations ("heads") run side by side, each learning to look for a different kind of relationship.`,
        h: `Instead of one big attention, the model splits its hidden vector into h smaller pieces (heads), e.g. 32 heads of 128 dimensions in a 4,096-dimension model. Each head has its own query, key and value projections, so it computes its own attention pattern. The heads' outputs are concatenated and mixed by a final projection. Different heads specialise: one tracks the previous word, one links pronouns to nouns, one follows syntax, one copies repeated names.`,
        u: `Every Transformer layer uses multi-head attention. You meet it in practice when reading model cards (number of heads, head dimension), when estimating KV-cache memory (it depends on the number of key/value heads), and when comparing efficient variants such as multi-query and grouped-query attention, which change how many key/value heads exist. It also matters for tensor parallelism: heads are split across GPUs when a model is too big for one card, so head counts must divide evenly.`,
        p: `A single attention pattern has to choose one way of weighting tokens. Real text needs many at once: grammatical structure, coreference, topic, position. Several heads let the model capture all of these in the same layer without them interfering, which is a big part of why Transformers understand language so well. Interpretability research has found specific heads for copying, induction (repeating patterns seen earlier) and position tracking, which shows how heads divide the work. Removing many heads hurts quality, which confirms that the variety matters.`,
        e: `A model with 4,096 hidden dimensions and 32 heads gives each head 128 dimensions. When it reads "Asha leads the payments team; she joined in 2021", one head strongly links "she" to "Asha", another links "joined" to "2021", and another keeps track of sentence boundaries. The combined output gives the next layer a vector for "she" that already means "Asha, the payments lead". The same mechanism lets an answer model connect a question word to the right sentence in a source.` },
      { t: `Query / Key / Value Vectors`,
        s: `Three learned versions of every token: the query asks "what am I looking for?", the key says "what do I contain?", the value is "what I pass on if chosen".`,
        h: `Each token's vector x is multiplied by three learned matrices: Q = xW_Q, K = xW_K, V = xW_V. Attention weights come from how well a token's query matches every token's key; the output is the weighted sum of the values. During generation, the keys and values of all earlier tokens never change, so servers store them in the KV cache and only compute Q, K and V for the newest token. The dot product of a query and a key is a similarity score; scaling by the square root of the dimension keeps scores in a stable range before softmax.`,
        u: `This vocabulary appears whenever you discuss attention, the KV cache, multi-query/grouped-query attention, or LoRA fine-tuning (LoRA is often applied to the q_proj and v_proj matrices). It is also the basis for estimating GPU/CPU memory for long contexts, because every stored token costs one key and one value vector per layer. When you read that a model has '16 query heads and 2 key/value heads', you are reading Q/K/V design choices that directly change memory use and speed.`,
        p: `Separating "what I'm looking for" from "what I offer" and "what I contain" lets the model learn flexible relationships: a token can match others for one reason (key) but pass on different information (value). Understanding Q/K/V also explains why long contexts use so much memory — the cached K and V grow with every token. It also explains why different tokens can attend to the same word for different reasons. For example, 'leave' might match 'annual' through one head's keys and 'days' through another's.`,
        e: `Think of a library. Your question is the query; the labels on the books are the keys; the contents are the values. You compare your question with every label, pick the best-matching books with weights, and read a blend of their contents. In a 36-layer model with 2 key/value heads of 128 dimensions in fp16, each token stores 2 × 36 × 2 × 128 × 2 bytes ≈ 37 KB of keys and values, so 4,000 tokens of context need about 150 MB.` },
      { t: `Feed-Forward (MLP) Layers`,
        s: `After attention, every token passes through a small neural network on its own — this is where much of the model's knowledge is stored.`,
        h: `Each layer has a position-wise feed-forward network: two or three linear layers with a non-linearity (GELU or SwiGLU) that expand the vector about 3–4× and project it back. It is applied to each token independently. Attention mixes information between tokens; the MLP transforms each token's representation and acts like a big learned lookup of patterns and facts. About two-thirds of a dense model's parameters live in these layers.`,
        u: `You meet MLP layers when counting parameters, when choosing LoRA target modules (gate_proj, up_proj, down_proj), and in Mixture-of-Experts models, which replace the single MLP with many "expert" MLPs and a router. Research on where models store facts also points at these layers, which helps explain why fine-tuning can change factual behaviour. When a model is quantised, these large MLP matrices are where most of the memory savings come from.`,
        p: `Attention alone only moves information between positions; it does not do much computation on each token. The MLP adds the capacity to transform features and store associations learned from training data, which is what lets the model "know" things and combine them. Without enough MLP capacity a model can route information well but cannot hold many facts or patterns. Bigger MLPs (or more experts in MoE models) generally mean more knowledge. They are also what makes each layer expensive to run, which is why MoE activates only some of them per token.`,
        e: `In a model with 4,096-dimension hidden vectors, each token's vector is expanded to about 11,000 dimensions inside the MLP, passed through SwiGLU, and projected back to 4,096. A Mixture-of-Experts model like Mixtral has 8 such MLPs per layer and sends each token through only 2 of them, so it has many more parameters but similar compute per token. Because so many parameters live in the MLPs, LoRA adapters on gate, up and down projections often teach domain style better than adapters on attention alone.` },
      { t: `Residual Connections`,
        s: `Each layer adds its output to its input (x + layer(x)) instead of replacing it, keeping a running "stream" of information.`,
        h: `Every attention block and every MLP block is wrapped as x = x + block(norm(x)). The vector flowing through the model is called the residual stream; each layer reads it and writes a small update. Gradients during training can flow straight back through the additions, so very deep networks (30–100+ layers) train stably. Information from early layers is never lost, only refined. This additive design is also why a model can skip or prune some layers with only a small loss of quality.`,
        u: `Residuals are part of every Transformer. They matter when you read about model internals, interpretability, or techniques like layer dropping and early exits. For practitioners, they explain why models can be so deep and why adding adapters (LoRA) as small additive updates works well — the whole architecture is built on adding small changes to a stream. When you debug strange model behaviour with interpretability tools, you inspect the residual stream at different layers to see what the model 'knows' at each point.`,
        p: `Without residual connections, very deep networks suffer from vanishing gradients: the training signal fades before reaching early layers, so they barely learn. Residuals solved this (first in ResNets for images) and made today's deep language models trainable. Residuals also make the model robust: if a layer learns something unhelpful, its small update does little harm, because the original information still passes through. This stability is one reason Transformers can be scaled to hundreds of billions of parameters. Almost every deep architecture since 2016 uses them.`,
        e: `A 36-layer model processes "How many leave days carry over?". Layer 1 adds basic word information to the stream, middle layers add the link between "carry over" and "leave days", later layers add the decision to look for a number in the sources. Each layer only edits the stream a little, and the final layer's stream is turned into next-token probabilities. Interpretability tools that 'read' the residual stream at each layer can show the model's best next-token guess slowly sharpening, layer by layer.` },
      { t: `Layer Normalization (Pre-LN / RMSNorm)`,
        s: `Rescaling each token's vector to a stable size inside every layer, so training and inference stay numerically stable.`,
        h: `LayerNorm subtracts the mean and divides by the standard deviation of a vector, then applies learned scale and shift. RMSNorm (used by Llama, Qwen, Mistral) only divides by the root-mean-square, which is cheaper and works as well. "Pre-LN" applies the normalisation before the attention and MLP blocks (x + block(norm(x))), which trains far more stably than the original "Post-LN" design. The small epsilon value in the formula prevents division by zero for near-zero vectors.`,
        u: `It is part of every model you use; you see it in model configs (rms_norm_eps) and it matters when converting, quantising or fine-tuning models — mixing up normalisation settings breaks outputs. It also explains some precision choices (norms are often kept in higher precision during quantisation). If you write custom inference code, implementing RMSNorm with the wrong epsilon or in low precision is a classic source of garbage output. Libraries like llama.cpp keep norms in higher precision for this reason.`,
        p: `In deep networks, vector values can grow or shrink layer after layer until they explode or vanish, making training unstable and outputs nonsensical. Normalisation keeps them in a healthy range so learning rates can be higher and deep stacks remain trainable. Post-LN Transformers needed careful learning-rate warm-up and still diverged at large scale; Pre-LN with RMSNorm made training large models much more reliable. It also shortened training time, because higher learning rates became safe. This small design choice is part of why modern open models train so consistently.`,
        e: `A Llama-style block computes h = x + Attention(RMSNorm(x)) and then out = h + MLP(RMSNorm(h)). If a token's vector has very large values after some layer, RMSNorm scales it back to unit size before the next attention, so one unusual token cannot destabilise the whole computation. Engineers converting a model to GGUF must keep the norm weights and epsilon exactly as configured; a wrong epsilon can produce fluent-looking but wrong text across every answer.` },
      { t: `Causal (Autoregressive) Masking`,
        s: `In decoder models each token may only attend to earlier tokens — never to future ones.`,
        h: `Before the softmax, a triangular mask sets the attention scores of all future positions to minus infinity, so their weights become zero. During training this lets the model learn to predict every next token of a sequence in parallel without seeing the answer. During generation it matches reality: the model only has the tokens written so far, predicts one more, appends it, and continues. Encoder models do not use this mask, which is why they cannot generate text but can understand both directions.`,
        u: `Every decoder-only LLM uses causal masking. It explains several practical facts: why generation is sequential (one token at a time), why the prompt can be processed in parallel (prefill) but the answer cannot, and why instructions placed after the question are still visible only to tokens that come after them. It also explains the KV cache: since earlier tokens never see later ones, their keys and values never change and can be stored and reused.`,
        p: `Without the mask, a model in training could simply look at the next token instead of predicting it, learning nothing useful. The mask forces genuine prediction and gives a model that can generate text left to right. It also makes training very efficient: one sequence of 4,000 tokens gives 4,000 prediction examples at once, because each position predicts its own next token under the mask. That is how models learn from trillions of tokens. The same masking at inference guarantees the model never peeks at text it has not generated yet.`,
        e: `For the sequence "Annual leave is 24 days", when predicting the token after "is", the model sees "Annual", "leave", "is" only; the mask hides "24" and "days". In a RAG prompt the sources come before the question, so when the model writes the answer, every answer token can attend to all sources and the question above it. This ordering is one reason RAG prompts usually put sources before the question and answer: everything the answer needs must come earlier in the sequence.`,
        c: `
import numpy as np
n = 5
mask = np.triu(np.ones((n, n)), k=1).astype(bool)   # True above the diagonal = future
scores = np.random.randn(n, n)
scores[mask] = -np.inf                              # softmax gives these weight 0` },
    ]},
    { id: "1.2", title: "Tokens, Embeddings & Positions", items: [
      { t: `Tokenizer (BPE / WordPiece / SentencePiece)`,
        s: `The algorithm that splits text into tokens using a vocabulary learned from data.`,
        h: `<b>BPE</b> (byte-pair encoding) starts from single characters or bytes and repeatedly merges the most frequent adjacent pair into a new token until the vocabulary is full; used by GPT, Llama and Qwen. <b>WordPiece</b> (BERT) is similar but picks merges that most increase likelihood and marks word-internal pieces with "##". <b>SentencePiece</b> treats text as a raw character stream including spaces (shown as "▁"), so it works for any language without pre-splitting words; it can use BPE or a unigram model.`,
        u: `Tokenizers decide how many tokens your chunks, prompts and answers use, which drives chunk sizing, context budgets, cost and latency. Always count tokens with the tokenizer of the model you are sizing for: the embedding model's for chunks, the LLM's for prompts. Multilingual RAG especially needs this, because the same sentence can take very different token counts in different languages and tokenizers.`,
        p: `A fixed word vocabulary cannot handle new words, typos, names, code or many languages — unknown words become a useless "UNK". Subword tokenizers can represent any text as known pieces, so models handle rare words and new product names gracefully. Tokenizers also affect cost directly: API pricing is per token, so a language or format that tokenises poorly costs more for the same content. Tokenization quirks explain odd model behaviour too, like trouble counting letters or handling numbers split into pieces. For RAG, mismatched tokenizers between how you size chunks and what the model uses lead to silent truncation.`,
        e: `"unbelievably" may become "un" + "belie" + "vably"; "E-4012" may become "E", "-", "40", "12". A 400-token chunk of English policy text is about 300 words, but the same limit holds far fewer words of Tamil with an English-centric tokenizer. This platform counts chunk tokens with the e5 tokenizer so no chunk exceeds the embedder's 512-token limit. Try it yourself: the same paragraph encoded by two different tokenizers can differ by 30% or more in token count.` },
      { t: `Vocabulary Size`,
        s: `How many different tokens a tokenizer knows — typically 32,000 to 250,000.`,
        h: `A larger vocabulary contains more whole words and multi-character pieces, especially for non-English scripts and code, so text is encoded in fewer tokens. The cost is a larger embedding table and output layer (vocabulary × hidden size parameters each) and a slightly slower final softmax. Modern multilingual models moved from 32k to 100k–250k vocabularies for efficiency across languages. The vocabulary is fixed when the tokenizer is trained; adding new tokens later requires resizing and training the embedding table.`,
        u: `Compare vocabulary sizes when choosing models for multilingual or code-heavy RAG: a model with a richer vocabulary for your languages fits more text into the same context window and costs less per document. It also matters for fine-tuning (adding tokens) and for estimating the parameter count of small models, where the embedding table can be a large share. If your domain has many unusual terms (drug names, part numbers), check how they tokenise: terms split into many pieces are harder for both embedders and LLMs.`,
        p: `Small English-centric vocabularies split other languages into many tiny pieces, so Hindi or Tamil text can need 2–4× more tokens than English. That means smaller effective context, higher cost and often worse quality for those users. Because context windows and prices are counted in tokens, an inefficient vocabulary quietly reduces how much evidence fits in a RAG prompt and raises cost per question. It can also lower quality, because the model must assemble meaning from many tiny pieces. Choosing a model whose vocabulary fits your languages is a cheap, high-impact decision.`,
        e: `Qwen2.5 uses a vocabulary of about 151,000 tokens and Llama 3 about 128,000, versus 32,000 for Llama 2. A Hindi HR policy that needed ~3,000 tokens with a 32k vocabulary might need well under 2,000 with a large multilingual vocabulary, letting more sources fit into the same prompt. For mixed English–Hindi support tickets, the larger vocabulary also means the same 4,096-token context window holds roughly half again as many tickets.` },
      { t: `Token Embeddings`,
        s: `A learned vector for each token in the vocabulary — the first layer of the model.`,
        h: `The tokenizer turns text into token IDs; each ID selects one row of the embedding matrix (vocabulary size × hidden size), giving a vector such as 2,048 numbers. At this point the vector is the same for "bank" in every sentence; attention layers then mix in context so "river bank" and "bank account" end up different. Many models tie this matrix with the output layer that scores the next token. Their size is vocabulary × hidden dimension, so a 151k-token vocabulary with 2,048 dimensions is about 300 million parameters — a large share of a 3B model.`,
        u: `Token embeddings are internal to every model. Do not confuse them with the sentence embeddings used for search: sentence embeddings are produced by an embedding model after all its layers and pooling. Knowing the difference avoids a common interview mistake and explains why you cannot take an LLM's first-layer vectors and use them for retrieval. In interviews, say clearly: token embeddings are the model's input lookup table; sentence embeddings for search come from a model trained for similarity, after every layer.`,
        p: `Neural networks work with numbers, not text. The embedding table turns discrete token IDs into dense vectors where similar tokens can start close together, giving the network a useful starting point for building meaning. They also let the model share knowledge between related tokens: pieces that appear in similar contexts end up with similar vectors, so the model generalises to rare words. When you add new tokens for fine-tuning, their embeddings start random and must be trained, which is why adding tokens needs care. The embedding table is also tied to the vocabulary, so changing tokenizers means retraining it.`,
        e: `The token "leave" maps to row 12,345 of the embedding table, a 2,048-number vector. In "annual leave" and "leave the building" it starts identical; after a few attention layers the first becomes a "time-off" meaning and the second a "depart" meaning, because each attended to different neighbours. That is why you never build a retrieval index from raw token embeddings: they carry word identity, not the sentence's meaning.` },
      { t: `Positional Encoding`,
        s: `Information about where each token is in the sequence — needed because attention by itself ignores order.`,
        h: `Attention compares sets of tokens; without position information "dog bites man" and "man bites dog" look identical. The original Transformer added fixed sine and cosine waves of different frequencies to each token embedding. BERT and GPT-2 learned one vector per absolute position. Modern LLMs mostly use relative schemes applied inside attention, such as RoPE (rotations) or ALiBi (distance penalties), which generalise better to long inputs.`,
        u: `Position schemes determine a model's context length and how well it handles inputs longer than it was trained on. When a model card mentions RoPE scaling, YaRN or ALiBi, it is talking about positions. In RAG this matters for long prompts with many sources and for long-context models that promise 128k tokens. It also affects how well a model handles very long chat histories in agents and assistants.`,
        p: `Order carries meaning: who did what to whom, which number belongs to which row, which rule has which exception. Without positions the model would treat text as a bag of words and misread sentences. Position schemes also decide what happens past the training length: some models degrade gently, others produce nonsense. That limit is often the real cap on how many retrieved chunks or history turns you can send. Choosing models with robust position handling avoids surprises when prompts grow. It is also why context extension requires changing position settings, not just accepting more tokens.`,
        e: `In "Contractors do not get paid leave; employees get 24 days", positions let the model know that "not" belongs to the contractor clause and "24 days" to employees. With learned absolute positions limited to 2,048, a model breaks on longer prompts; with RoPE and scaling it can stretch to 32k tokens or more. Tables are a good test: shuffle the rows' positions and a model loses which value belongs to which header — the same failure positions prevent.` },
      { t: `RoPE (Rotary Position Embedding)`,
        s: `Encodes position by rotating the query and key vectors by an angle that grows with the token's position.`,
        h: `The query and key vectors are split into pairs of numbers, and each pair is rotated by an angle equal to position × a frequency (different per pair). When attention takes the dot product of a rotated query and a rotated key, the result depends on the difference between their positions, so the model naturally sees relative distance. No extra position vectors are added. Llama, Qwen, Mistral and most open LLMs use RoPE.`,
        u: `You meet RoPE in model configs (rope_theta, rope_scaling) and whenever you extend context length: techniques like position interpolation, NTK scaling and YaRN all work by adjusting RoPE's frequencies. If you serve a model with a longer context than it was trained for, the RoPE scaling settings must be correct or quality collapses. It also appears in fine-tuning tools, where you may set rope_scaling to train with longer sequences.`,
        p: `Absolute position embeddings do not generalise beyond the training length and do not directly capture "how far apart" two tokens are. RoPE captures relative distance cleanly and allows context extension with modest extra training. Before RoPE, most models either used absolute positions (bad beyond training length) or complex relative schemes that slowed attention. RoPE gives relative position information with no extra parameters and almost no extra compute. Its simple maths made context-extension tricks possible, which is how many open models went from 4k to 128k tokens. That flexibility is why it became the standard.`,
        e: `Two tokens 10 positions apart interact the same way whether they sit at positions 5 and 15 or 10,005 and 10,015, because only the angle difference matters. A model trained at 32k tokens can be run at 128k by applying YaRN scaling to its RoPE frequencies, as Qwen2.5's documentation describes for long-context use. A config line like rope_theta=1000000 (instead of 10000) is one way models are prepared for longer contexts.` },
      { t: `ALiBi`,
        s: `Attention with Linear Biases: instead of position vectors, it subtracts a penalty from attention scores that grows with the distance between tokens.`,
        h: `For each attention head, ALiBi adds −m × (distance between query and key) to the attention score before softmax, where m is a fixed slope that differs per head. Nearby tokens are favoured, distant ones are penalised more strongly in some heads than others. Because the bias is a simple function of distance, the model extrapolates reasonably to sequences longer than those seen in training. No position information is added to token embeddings at all; the bias is the only signal about order.`,
        u: `ALiBi was used by models such as BLOOM and MPT. Today RoPE is more common, but ALiBi appears in interviews and comparisons of position methods, especially when discussing length extrapolation (running a model on longer inputs than it was trained on). It is still a useful design reference when you compare how different models behave on long documents, and some efficient encoders and research models use it. Knowing ALiBi vs RoPE also helps you read papers and model cards critically.`,
        p: `Learned absolute positions fail beyond the training length, and early RoPE models degraded quickly past it. ALiBi offered a simple way to train on short sequences and still work on longer ones without retraining. Training on long sequences is expensive, so a method that trains on short sequences and still works on longer ones saves a lot of compute. ALiBi also needs no position parameters, keeping models simpler. Its weakness is that the strong distance penalty can make very distant tokens hard to use, which is why RoPE with scaling won for most chat LLMs.`,
        e: `A model trained with ALiBi on 2,048-token sequences can still produce sensible output on 4,000-token inputs, because the distance penalty for position 3,000 follows the same straight line it learned. Heads with small slopes keep long-range attention; heads with large slopes focus locally, like a mix of zoomed-in and zoomed-out views. In a long contract, ALiBi naturally favours nearby clauses; if the key definition is 20 pages earlier, retrieval that puts it close to the question helps such models a lot.` },
      { t: `Context Length Extension (YaRN, RoPE Scaling)`,
        s: `Techniques that let a model handle longer inputs than it was originally trained on.`,
        h: `With RoPE, positions beyond the training length produce rotation angles the model never saw. Position interpolation squeezes long positions into the trained range; NTK-aware scaling changes frequencies unevenly; YaRN combines frequency-dependent scaling with an attention temperature fix. Usually a short fine-tune on long documents follows. The model then accepts, say, 128k tokens instead of 8k. Serving engines read these settings from the model config; using a different scaling factor than the model was tuned with harms quality.`,
        u: `Relevant when you deploy long-context models or configure rope_scaling in vLLM/llama.cpp. For RAG it matters if you plan "put the whole document in the prompt" designs or long chat histories — but always test real quality at the length you need, because accepting tokens is not the same as using them well. Also check the effective context in your serving engine: Ollama defaults to a smaller context (num_ctx) than the model supports unless you set it.`,
        p: `Pretraining a model from scratch on very long sequences is extremely expensive. Extension methods give long context cheaply, so models can read long contracts, codebases or many retrieved sources at once. It lets one base model serve both short chat and long-document use cases. But extended models often use the far end of their window less reliably, and longer prompts are slower and costlier because of attention's quadratic cost. So extension solves 'does it fit' more than 'is it used well'. In RAG, selecting the right 2,000 tokens usually beats sending 100,000.`,
        e: `A model pretrained at 8k tokens is extended to 32k with position interpolation and 1–2 billion tokens of long-document fine-tuning. A needle-in-a-haystack test then shows it finds a hidden fact at 30k tokens; a multi-fact reasoning test may still show weaker accuracy past ~16k, which tells you to keep RAG contexts compact. In this platform the model supports long contexts, but we set num_ctx to 4,096 and keep sources near 1,200 tokens because CPU prefill is slow.` },
      { t: `Special Tokens (BOS, EOS, PAD)`,
        s: `Reserved tokens with a job: BOS marks the beginning, EOS marks the end, PAD fills empty space in batches.`,
        h: `The tokenizer adds BOS at the start of a sequence on some models. The model learns to produce EOS when an answer is complete, and generation stops there. PAD tokens make sequences in a batch the same length and are masked out of attention and loss. Chat models add more special tokens to mark roles and turns. These tokens have their own IDs and must be handled exactly as the model was trained. Some tokenizers add BOS automatically; adding it twice (once in the template, once by the tokenizer) is a subtle bug that slightly degrades output.`,
        u: `Special tokens matter when fine-tuning (wrong EOS handling creates models that never stop or stop too early), when batching (padding side and masks), and when serving (stop tokens). Most bugs where a fine-tuned model rambles forever or repeats the next user turn come from special-token mistakes. When serving, set stop tokens correctly so streaming ends cleanly; when batching embeddings, use attention masks so padding does not change vectors.`,
        p: `Without a clear end token the model cannot signal that it is finished; without masking, padding pollutes training. Correct special tokens give clean starts, clean stops and efficient batches. Mistakes here are common and hard to spot: a model that 'works' in a notebook but rambles in production often has a stop-token mismatch. Padding without masks changes embedding vectors, so identical texts get different embeddings in different batches. Getting special tokens right is cheap and prevents many hours of debugging.`,
        e: `Qwen marks turns with <code>&lt;|im_start|&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;|im_end|&gt;</code>; when the assistant writes <code>&lt;|im_end|&gt;</code>, Ollama stops generation. If you fine-tune without putting <code>&lt;|im_end|&gt;</code> at the end of each training answer, the tuned model may keep writing a fake "user:" turn after its answer. In embedding batches, forgetting the attention mask made short chunks' vectors depend on the longest chunk in the batch — fixed by passing the mask to the pooling step.` },
      { t: `Chat Templates (system / user / assistant)`,
        s: `The exact text format a chat model expects for turning a list of messages into one token sequence.`,
        h: `Each model family wraps messages in its own markers, e.g. <code>&lt;|im_start|&gt;system ... &lt;|im_end|&gt;</code> for Qwen or <code>[INST] ... [/INST]</code> for older Mistral models. The tokenizer usually ships the template, and <code>tokenizer.apply_chat_template()</code> produces the correct string, ending with the marker that asks the assistant to start writing. Servers such as Ollama and vLLM apply it automatically when you send a messages list.`,
        u: `Important whenever you call open models directly (raw completion endpoints), build fine-tuning data, or switch model families. Sending a chat model a plain string without its template, or the wrong family's template, measurably weakens instruction following — the model may ignore your system prompt or grounding rules. LiteLLM and most SDKs send a messages list and let the server apply the template, which is the safest approach.`,
        p: `Instruct models are trained on one specific layout of roles. If the prompt layout differs, the model does not recognise which part is a rule and which is the question, and quality drops silently. Template bugs are silent: there is no error, only worse answers. They often appear after switching models or when someone builds prompts as raw strings. Fine-tuning data in the wrong template teaches the model a format it will never see in production. Using the tokenizer's own template everywhere removes this whole class of problems.`,
        e: `The system message "Answer only from the sources" plus the user's question become one string with Qwen's markers and a final <code>&lt;|im_start|&gt;assistant</code>. Using the same template in fine-tuning data and at serving time keeps behaviour consistent. A team switching from Llama to Qwen kept Llama's [INST] markers in raw prompts; grounding rules were ignored until they switched to a messages list so the server applied Qwen's template. The fix took one line but recovered a large drop in instruction following.`,
        c: `
from transformers import AutoTokenizer
tok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained("Qwen/Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct")
text = tok.apply_chat_template(
    [{"role": "system", "content": "Answer only from the sources."},
     {"role": "user", "content": "How many leave days do I get?"}],
    tokenize=False, add_generation_prompt=True)
print(text)` },
      { t: `Logits & Softmax`,
        s: `Logits are the raw scores the model gives every vocabulary token for "next token"; softmax turns them into probabilities.`,
        h: `The last layer produces one number (logit) per vocabulary token, e.g. 151,000 numbers. Softmax computes p_i = exp(z_i / T) ÷ Σ exp(z_j / T), where T is the temperature, giving probabilities that sum to 1. Decoding strategies (greedy, top-p, top-k) then choose a token from this distribution. The log of a chosen token's probability (its logprob) is a useful confidence signal. Many APIs can return the top few alternatives with their logprobs for each generated token.`,
        u: `This is how temperature and sampling settings actually work, how classification-by-LLM can read scores for labels, and how confidence estimation or active retrieval (FLARE) detects uncertain tokens. Cross-encoder rerankers also output logits — which is why their scores are not probabilities and need calibration. Classifying by comparing the logits of label tokens (e.g. 'yes' vs 'no') is a cheap way to get calibrated-ish scores from an LLM judge.`,
        p: `A neural network outputs unbounded scores; to choose the next token, compare options or measure confidence, they must become a proper probability distribution. Softmax with temperature also gives a single knob to control randomness. It also explains why some outputs vary between runs: with sampling, lower-probability tokens are sometimes chosen. And it shows why raw scores from different models or rerankers cannot be compared directly — they are on different scales until normalised. Understanding logits helps you set thresholds and temperatures sensibly instead of by trial and error.`,
        e: `Logits [5.0, 3.0, 1.0] for the tokens "24", "20", "30" give probabilities of about 0.87, 0.12 and 0.02 at temperature 1. At temperature 2 they flatten to about 0.67, 0.24 and 0.09, so sampling might pick "20" — which is why grounded RAG answers use a low temperature like 0.1. A router asked to answer 'hr' or 'it' can read the logits of both tokens: 'hr' at 0.93 means a confident route, 0.55 means ask a clarifying question. That small check prevents many wrong routes.`,
        c: `
import numpy as np
def softmax(z, temperature=1.0):
    z = np.array(z) / temperature
    e = np.exp(z - z.max())
    return e / e.sum()
print(softmax([5.0, 3.0, 1.0]), softmax([5.0, 3.0, 1.0], temperature=2.0))` },
    ]},
    { id: "1.3", title: "Attention Variants & Efficiency", items: [
      { t: `Multi-Query Attention (MQA)`,
        s: `All attention heads share one set of keys and values, which makes the KV cache much smaller.`,
        h: `In normal multi-head attention every head has its own key and value projections, so the KV cache stores h keys and values per token per layer. MQA keeps a separate query per head but only one shared key and one shared value. The cache shrinks by the number of heads, memory bandwidth during decoding drops, and generation gets faster. Quality drops slightly because heads can no longer look for different things in different key spaces.`,
        u: `MQA appears in model architecture comparisons and in models optimised for fast inference (e.g. PaLM, Falcon). Understanding it helps you estimate memory for long contexts and many concurrent users, and explains why some models fit far more users per GPU than others of the same size. It is mostly a serving-efficiency choice; when you compare two models for a high-traffic RAG assistant, the attention design can matter as much as the parameter count. MQA models are attractive when many users share one GPU.`,
        p: `During decoding, each new token reads the whole KV cache from memory; with long prompts and many users the cache dominates memory and bandwidth, limiting batch size and speed. Sharing keys and values cuts that cost dramatically. Decoding is limited by memory bandwidth: every new token must read the entire KV cache. Shrinking the cache means each step reads less memory, so tokens come out faster, and more users fit at once. For chat services with long contexts, this can be the difference between one GPU and several.`,
        e: `A model with 32 heads and MQA stores 1 key/value head per layer instead of 32, so a 8,000-token conversation's cache is 32× smaller. The server can keep many more conversations in GPU memory at once, raising throughput for a busy support chatbot. Throughput benchmarks of MQA models typically show much higher tokens per second per GPU at large batch sizes than same-size multi-head models, with only a small quality gap. That is why inference-focused model designs adopted it early.` },
      { t: `Grouped-Query Attention (GQA)`,
        s: `A middle ground: groups of query heads share one key/value head, keeping most of the quality with a much smaller KV cache.`,
        h: `Query heads are divided into g groups; each group shares one key head and one value head. With 32 query heads and 8 groups, the cache is 4× smaller than full multi-head attention, while quality stays close to it — better than MQA's single shared head. Most current open models (Llama 3, Qwen2.5, Mistral) use GQA. Existing multi-head models can even be converted to GQA by averaging their key/value heads within each group and briefly continuing training.`,
        u: `Read num_key_value_heads in a model's config to estimate KV-cache size and how many tokens/users fit in memory. This is part of capacity planning when you self-host the answer model for RAG, especially with long contexts of retrieved sources. When comparing two 7B models, the one with fewer KV heads usually supports more concurrent long-context users on the same GPU. For CPU deployments like this platform, a smaller KV cache also means less RAM per conversation.`,
        p: `MQA saves the most memory but can cost quality; full multi-head attention keeps quality but uses lots of memory. GQA gives most of the savings with very little quality loss, which is why it became the default. Serving long RAG prompts to many users is limited by KV-cache memory, not just model weights. GQA cuts that memory by 4–8× with little quality loss, so the same hardware serves more users with longer contexts. That is why almost every new open model since 2023 uses it. It is a hidden reason modern models are cheaper to run.`,
        e: `Qwen2.5-3B uses 16 query heads and 2 key/value heads, 36 layers and 128-dimension heads. Its KV cache is about 37 KB per token in fp16 — roughly 150 MB for a 4,000-token RAG prompt — instead of about 300 KB per token if every query head had its own keys and values. With 8 KV heads instead of 32, a 70B model's 32k-token cache per user drops from tens of gigabytes to about a quarter of that. A single server can then hold several long conversations instead of one.` },
      { t: `FlashAttention`,
        s: `An exact attention algorithm that is much faster because it computes attention in small tiles inside fast GPU memory.`,
        h: `Standard attention writes the full n × n score matrix to slow GPU memory and reads it back for softmax and the value multiplication. FlashAttention splits queries, keys and values into blocks, computes softmax incrementally per block inside fast on-chip memory, and never stores the full matrix. The result is identical to normal attention, but with far less memory traffic, so it runs faster and uses less memory, especially for long sequences.`,
        u: `It is built into modern training and serving stacks (PyTorch scaled_dot_product_attention, vLLM, TGI, Hugging Face with flash_attention_2). You mostly "use" it by enabling it; knowing it explains why long-context training and serving became practical and why GPU type matters for speed. On older GPUs or CPUs it may not be available; serving engines fall back to other kernels, which is one reason the same model can be much slower on different hardware.`,
        p: `Attention was memory-bound: GPUs spent most of their time moving the huge score matrix rather than computing. For long sequences the matrix did not even fit. FlashAttention removed that bottleneck without approximations. Long-context training and serving were limited more by memory movement than by maths. FlashAttention made attention 2–4× faster in practice and cut memory from quadratic to linear in sequence length. That made long prompts, long documents and larger batches affordable. Without it, many of today's long-context models would be impractical to train or serve.`,
        e: `Fine-tuning a model on 8,000-token documents runs out of memory with standard attention on a 24 GB GPU, but fits with FlashAttention-2 and gradient checkpointing. In serving, vLLM uses FlashAttention kernels so the prefill of a 4,000-token RAG prompt takes a fraction of a second on a modern GPU. Enabling attn_implementation='flash_attention_2' in a Hugging Face fine-tuning script can roughly double training speed on supported GPUs, with identical results.` },
      { t: `Sliding Window Attention`,
        s: `Each token attends only to the last W tokens instead of to every previous token.`,
        h: `With window W (e.g. 4,096), attention cost becomes proportional to n × W instead of n². Information can still travel further than W because each layer passes it on: after L layers, a token can be influenced by tokens up to L × W positions back. Many models mix sliding-window layers with some full (global) attention layers to keep long-range recall. The KV cache can also be capped at W tokens for those layers, so memory stops growing after the window fills.`,
        u: `Used by Mistral 7B and in some layers of Gemma models. Relevant when you evaluate long-context models: a sliding-window model may handle long inputs cheaply but recall exact facts from far back less reliably than full attention, which matters if your RAG prompt puts key evidence early. It also suits streaming applications where only recent context matters. When you evaluate such a model for RAG, include questions whose answer sits at the start of a long prompt.`,
        p: `Full attention's quadratic cost makes long inputs slow and memory-hungry. Limiting each token to a local window makes long sequences affordable while keeping local language understanding strong. Memory and compute for long sequences grow quickly with full attention. A sliding window caps both, which makes long chat sessions and long documents cheaper to serve. The trade-off is weaker direct access to distant details. Hybrid layouts with some global layers recover much of that while keeping the savings.`,
        e: `With W = 4,096, token 10,000 directly attends to tokens 5,905–10,000. A fact at position 100 can still reach it indirectly through several layers, but a direct quote of an exact number from that far back is harder — so a RAG system keeps the most important sources close to the question. For chat, this behaves well: recent turns matter most, and older ones fade naturally. Measure it with a needle test at several depths before relying on long prompts.` },
      { t: `Sparse Attention`,
        s: `Attention that only connects selected pairs of tokens (local windows, strides, a few global tokens) instead of all pairs.`,
        h: `Patterns are chosen so that information can still flow across the sequence: Longformer uses a local window for every token plus a few "global" tokens that attend everywhere; BigBird adds random connections. The number of computed pairs grows roughly linearly with length, so very long documents become feasible. Global tokens act like summaries that every token can read from and write to, so distant parts of the document still exchange information in two steps.`,
        u: `Used in long-document encoders (legal, scientific) and some research LLMs. In RAG it is relevant for long-document classification or embedding models designed for thousands of tokens, and as background for interview questions about handling long inputs. It also inspired attention patterns inside modern long-context LLMs and is a common interview topic when discussing scaling to long inputs. Know at least Longformer and BigBird as examples.`,
        p: `Comparing every token with every other is wasteful for long documents where most pairs are irrelevant. Sparse patterns keep the useful connections at a fraction of the cost. Full attention over 10,000 tokens means 100 million scores per head per layer. Most of these pairs carry little information. Sparse patterns keep the important connections (local context plus a few global hubs) and make long-document models practical on ordinary hardware. The trade-off is less flexible long-range interaction than full attention.`,
        e: `A Longformer-based model classifies 10,000-token contracts: each token attends to its 512 neighbours, and the [CLS] token attends to everything, so the final classification still sees the whole contract without computing 100 million attention scores. The same idea helps long-document embedding: a model that reads a whole 8-page contract in one pass can produce a better summary vector than averaging page vectors.` },
      { t: `Linear Attention`,
        s: `Approximations of attention whose cost grows linearly, not quadratically, with sequence length.`,
        h: `Softmax attention computes softmax(QKᵀ)V, which needs the n × n matrix. Linear attention replaces softmax with a feature map φ so the product can be regrouped as φ(Q)(φ(K)ᵀV): the inner part is a small matrix independent of n, so cost is linear. It can also be computed like a recurrent network with a fixed-size state. Quality is usually lower than softmax attention on recall-heavy tasks. That fixed state is both its strength (memory never grows) and its weakness (details get blurred).`,
        u: `Mostly research and specialised long-sequence models; the ideas live on in state-space models and hybrid architectures. Knowing it helps you understand why some "infinite context" models are cheap but weaker at retrieving exact details — important when judging them for RAG. You are unlikely to deploy it directly, but newer architectures (RWKV, RetNet, Mamba-style layers) build on the same idea of a compressed running state.`,
        p: `Quadratic attention limits sequence length and makes long inputs expensive. Linear attention promised constant memory per token and linear time for very long streams. The promise was handling arbitrarily long sequences in linear time and constant memory, which would make long documents and endless streams cheap. In practice the compressed state loses detail, so precise lookup of old information suffers. This trade-off — efficiency vs exact recall — is exactly why RAG still matters even as architectures change. Retrieval brings exact text back into a short context.`,
        e: `A linear-attention model processes a 100,000-token log stream with constant memory, summarising trends well, but when asked for the exact error code at line 1,200 it often fails — a reason to keep RAG (retrieve the exact line) even with such models. The usual production pattern is to pair such models with retrieval: a search step finds the exact lines, and the model reasons over them. Benchmarks of long-range recall show this gap clearly against softmax attention.` },
      { t: `KV Cache`,
        s: `Stored keys and values of all earlier tokens, so generating each new token does not recompute the whole prompt.`,
        h: `During prefill the model computes keys and values for every prompt token in every layer and stores them. During decoding it computes Q, K and V only for the new token, appends K and V to the cache, and attends over the whole cache. Memory per token = 2 (K and V) × layers × KV heads × head dimension × bytes per number. Servers also reuse cached prefixes across requests (prefix caching). Some servers also quantise the cache (8-bit) to fit more tokens.`,
        u: `The KV cache decides how many users and how long contexts fit on your hardware, and it is the reason prompt caching saves time. When sizing a self-hosted RAG answer model, compute KV memory for your typical prompt (instructions + sources + history) times concurrent users. It also explains why long chat histories slow every turn and why trimming history helps. Serving engines like vLLM manage the cache in blocks (PagedAttention) to fit more users.`,
        p: `Without a cache, generating token n would reprocess all n−1 previous tokens, making generation quadratic and very slow. The cache makes each new token cost roughly the same, at the price of memory. The cost is memory: for long contexts and many users, the KV cache can exceed the model weights. That is why techniques like GQA, quantised KV caches, paged memory and prefix caching exist. Planning a deployment without estimating KV memory leads to out-of-memory errors or far fewer concurrent users than expected. Estimate it before buying hardware.`,
        e: `For qwen2.5:3b (36 layers, 2 KV heads, 128 dims, fp16): 2 × 36 × 2 × 128 × 2 bytes ≈ 37 KB per token. A 4,096-token context needs ≈ 151 MB per conversation; 20 concurrent conversations need ≈ 3 GB of KV cache on top of the model weights. Adding a 2,000-token chat history to each request would add ≈ 75 MB per user, which is why assistants trim or summarise history. Quantising the KV cache to 8 bits roughly halves these numbers.`,
        c: `
def kv_cache_bytes(tokens, layers, kv_heads, head_dim, bytes_per=2):
    return 2 * layers * kv_heads * head_dim * bytes_per * tokens
print(round(kv_cache_bytes(4096, 36, 2, 128) / 1e6), "MB per 4k-token context")` },
      { t: `Attention Sinks`,
        s: `Models put a lot of attention on the very first tokens; keeping those tokens in the cache keeps long streaming generation stable.`,
        h: `Softmax forces attention weights to sum to 1, so heads with "nothing important to look at" dump their weight on the first tokens, which act as sinks. The StreamingLLM paper (2023) showed that if you slide the KV cache window and evict those first tokens, quality collapses; keeping a few initial "sink" tokens plus a recent window lets models generate very long streams with bounded memory. The sink tokens carry little meaning; they just give attention a stable place to rest.`,
        u: `Relevant for long-running chat sessions or agents served with a bounded KV cache, and for understanding odd attention patterns in interpretability. Some serving frameworks implement sink-aware cache eviction for streaming use cases. It also explains interpretability findings where the first token receives huge attention weights without carrying meaning. If you build long-running agents, prefer serving engines that handle cache eviction this way.`,
        p: `Naive sliding windows over the KV cache (drop the oldest tokens) unexpectedly break models. Keeping sink tokens fixes this cheaply. Bounded-memory generation is needed for very long sessions on fixed hardware. Before this finding, sliding the cache window made models fail in mysterious ways after a few thousand tokens. Keeping just four sink tokens restored stable output, a tiny change with a big effect. It also deepened understanding of how softmax attention behaves.`,
        e: `A monitoring assistant streams commentary for hours. The server keeps tokens 0–3 plus the latest 4,000 tokens in the cache; generation stays fluent indefinitely, whereas evicting the first tokens made the output degrade into nonsense after the window first slid. Some models are now trained with a dedicated sink token so this behaviour is intentional and predictable. The cost was only four extra cached tokens per conversation.` },
      { t: `Quadratic Attention Cost (O(n²))`,
        s: `Standard attention compares every token with every other, so doubling the input roughly quadruples the attention work.`,
        h: `For n tokens, each head in each layer computes n × n scores. The feed-forward parts grow only linearly, so for short prompts attention is a small share of cost, but for long prompts it dominates. FlashAttention fixes the memory problem, not the quadratic compute, and decode-time attention also grows with the cache length. Decode time also grows with context, because each new token attends over the whole cached prompt.`,
        u: `This is why long RAG prompts increase latency and cost faster than you expect, why context budgets matter, and why "just put everything in a 128k context" is expensive. It is a standard interview point when discussing long context vs retrieval. Use it to explain trade-offs with numbers: sending fewer, better chunks is both cheaper and often more accurate. When someone proposes 'send the whole document every time', estimate the extra cost per question first.`,
        p: `Teams increase top-k and context size to "help" the model and are surprised by slow, costly answers. Understanding the quadratic term explains the trade-off and motivates selecting fewer, better chunks. Context size is one of the biggest cost drivers in production LLM systems, both in latency and money. Teams that ignore it see bills and response times grow as they add more sources. Knowing the scaling helps you set budgets: more tokens must earn their place through better answers. It also explains why reranking and compression pay off.`,
        e: `Going from 2,000 to 8,000 prompt tokens is 4× more tokens but about 16× more attention computation. In a RAG system that moved from 3 to 12 sources, time to first token rose far more than 4×, so the team returned to 5 reranked sources with no loss in answer quality. The same effect appears in API bills: input tokens are billed per token, and the latency of very long prompts grows even faster. Measured latency, not intuition, should decide the context size.` },
      { t: `State Space Models (Mamba) & Hybrids`,
        s: `Sequence models that keep a fixed-size running state instead of attending to every past token — linear time and constant memory per token.`,
        h: `A state space model updates a hidden state as each token arrives, like a recurrent network but designed to train in parallel. Mamba makes the update depend on the input ("selective"), so it can choose what to remember. Cost grows linearly with length and there is no growing KV cache. They are weaker at exact recall of details from far back, so hybrids (e.g. Jamba) mix many Mamba layers with a few attention layers.`,
        u: `Relevant when evaluating new long-context or efficient models, especially for very long inputs (logs, genomics, long documents) or edge devices. For RAG, a hybrid can read long contexts cheaply, but exact quoting of retrieved facts still relies on its attention layers — test retrieval-style questions before adopting one. They are also attractive on CPUs and phones because there is no KV cache that grows with every token.`,
        p: `Transformers' KV cache and quadratic cost make very long sequences expensive. SSMs offer near-constant memory and fast generation, at some cost in precise recall. As contexts grow to hundreds of thousands of tokens, Transformer memory and compute become the bottleneck. SSMs and hybrids promise long inputs at much lower cost. Their known weakness — imprecise recall of specific old details — is exactly where retrieval helps, so RAG and SSM-based models complement each other.`,
        e: `A hybrid model with mostly Mamba layers and one attention layer every eight summarises a 200,000-token log quickly with little memory. When asked for an exact timestamp buried early in the log, its few attention layers help it find it — pure Mamba models of the same size often miss such details. For RAG, the safe pattern remains: retrieve the exact facts, then let any architecture write the answer.` },
    ]},
  ],
});
